Z||000005||Hock||05-06-2000||10:01 PM||||100% SURE. IF YOU LOOK AT YOUR TECH. MANUALS THEY SHOULD STATE THAT. MY ENGINE WAS THE ORIGINAL FOR THE CAR. OTHER THINGS TO LOOK AT WOULD BE THINGS LIKE REBUILD KITS FROM PLACES LIKE NORTHERN AUTO PARTS, OR EVEN CHECK OUT A LOCAL PARTS STORE. THE FIERO STORE EVEN HAS THE 85 LISTED AS ROLLER CAM. SO THAT IS THE ONLY INFO I CAN BACK THIS STATEMENT UP WITH. LET ME KNOW WHAT YOU FIND. AND GOOD LUCK ON YOUR DIG!!!!||||reg||14
Z||000006||Zman302||05-06-2000||11:31 PM||||I belive that the 85 model was a mid yr change.  I have many customers that come in my store that have 85 fieros and they purchase flat tappet cams to replace the flat tappet that came out.  Also, i know a few people that have 85's that were purchased new and they have flat tappets.  hope this may help some.||||reg||1
Z||000007||theogre||05-07-2000||03:03 PM||||first of all, hock, turn off the caps or fix your keyboard. They cost a whole 15$.<P>No one should ever believe everything in a manual or cross reference lists. They are commonly wrong. I have close to 1000 pages of updates and corrections just to the 1987 GM manuals. GM, like the rest of the big 3, constantly changes things in production without any official notice at all. TSB's on such changes may not show up for weeks to years if at all.<P>If I believed every parts book I'd swear for sure my 87 4 cyl had a distributor, a single coil, and a PCV filter under the air cleaner neck.||||reg||1
